- id: when-then | format: Say this, not that | topic: When-Then: "When shoes are on, then we go to the park" instead of threats

PART 2 — 3 FRESH IDEAS of your own, clearly different from every topic and title in this message (Part 1, the bank list, the already-made and already-shown lists), in these formats, one each, in this order: say_this, problem_fix, say_this. The formats:
- named_method (Named method): a catchy named method with 3-4 steps ("The 2-Choice Rule")
- say_this (Say this, not that): phrases to stop saying and what to say instead
- lola_science (Grandma said, science says): an old wives' tale or old rule known in many countries, and what doctors or experts say now
- scene_lesson (Scene, pivot, lesson): a real everyday moment, what is really going on, and the words that help
- problem_fix (Problem, why, fix): one specific problem, the child's-eye reason, and simple fixes
Each fresh topic is specific (a concrete problem, method, old saying or exact phrase, never a vague one), at most 90 characters, in plain words, written like the topics in Part 1. Health ideas (fever, illness, sleep, feeding, how a baby grows) are allowed: set "health" to true and keep them to general comfort and care (no medicine names, doses or diagnoses). Otherwise "health" is false.

THE HOOK (every idea): the reel's first spoken line, heard in the first half-second. At most 12 words (aim for 6-10). It talks to the mom ('you', 'your toddler', or a direct 'Stop saying…' / 'Try…'), names the SPECIFIC problem, method, old saying or exact phrase, and promises something concrete. Never a greeting or intro ('Hey moms', 'Today…'), never a call to action (follow, like, comment, share, save), never 'don't worry' or 'don't feel bad', never I / we / my (words a child or the mom says go in double quotes: '"Mommy, I need to poop!" Right as you sit down?'), never 'forever', 'proven' or a fear line. COUNT the words: 13 is too many. Examples of the style: 'Stop saying "calm down". Try the 5-Word Rule instead.' — 'Your toddler hits when he's mad? He's out of words.' — 'Grandma said wet hair gives you a cold. Doctors disagree.'
THE WHY: at most 15 words: what she learns or can do after watching, in plain words.

ALREADY MADE — do not repeat or rephrase these:
- Bedtime stalling

ALREADY IN THE TOPIC BANK — fresh ideas must not repeat or rephrase these (or reuse their method names):
